      explanation: "**Choice D is correct.** If the reservoir traps the sediment while waves keep stripping the delta's front, the losses continue without replacement, so the delta can shrink.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n- How to spot it: a fill-the-blank conclusion must follow only from what is stated. Restate the passage's balance, then remove the piece the passage says is removed.\n- The passage sets up a balance: the sea takes sediment off the delta's front while fresh mud arrives from upstream.\n- A dam knocks out one side of that balance, since \"most of the sediment\" settles on the reservoir floor \"rather than continuing downstream.\" With removal continuing and delivery cut, the delta loses ground.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- A: Clearer water means less sediment delivered, which would starve the delta rather than make it grow.\n- B: The passage gives no reason for waves and currents to weaken; it is the supply that changes.\n- C: How fast the reservoir fills with water is not addressed, and it says nothing about the delta.",
